  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through next Monday)\nIssued at 134 PM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nMid and upper level high pressure remain centered over the\nsouthwestern U.S. on Wednesday. We do expect to see the flow aloft\ntrend to a more northwesterly direction in response to the\nlocation of the high pressure ridge. There is still some signs\nthat convection to our north could send a weak outflow boundary\nsouthward late Wednesday. Our confidence is low as models keep any\nwind shift/outflow boundary to our north. We will monitor for the\npossibility of adding some low rain chances to portions of the\nsouthern Edwards Plateau and northern Hill Country, but will keep\nthe forecast dry for now. \n\nWe will continue to mention low rain chances in the forecast for\nmost of the region Thursday into Friday as northwest flow aloft\nsends a few weak disturbances into the region. Any convection that\nmanages to develop will be high-based and could pose a gusty wind\nthreat with the stronger cells. While temperatures will continue\nto remain above normal during the mid-week period, an increase in\ncloud cover and slightly lower 850mb temperatures will hopefully\nkeep temperatures and heat index values below Heat Advisory\ncriteria. \n\nTowards the end of this week and into early next week, the flow\naloft should trend back to a more north to northeasterly direction\nsignaling an end to rain chances. Temperature trends from the\nECMWF and GFS MOS guidance are on the uptick and if this\ncontinues, our current forecast temperatures will be a little low.",
